// # DINNER_TOKENOMICS_REFERENCE.oot
//
// Logline: documents the `// ## Tokenomics` header so an agent knows which form is canonical and which is compressed short-form.
//
// Setup: DINNER.oot is the long-form canonical version. This file explains the header, the format spec, when to use each form, and the compression rules that produced the short-form.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Why Tokenomics matters — context-window economics.
//   2. Format spec — the exact header lines.
//   3. Canonical vs short-form — when to use each.
//   4. Compression rules — how short-form is produced.
//   5. Example and trade-offs.

// ## 1. Why Tokenomics matters
//
// LLMs have a limited context window. A long-form file eats tokens that could be spent on the task. The Tokenomics header tells the agent which file is canonical (long, human-readable) and which is the compressed short-form (token-cheap). Canonical is the source of truth. Short-form exists only to save tokens at read time.

// ## 2. Format spec
//
// A canonical file has at the top:
//
//   // ## Tokenomics
//   //
//   //   canonical:    yes   # this is the long-form canonical version
//   //   short-form:   <path-to-compact-form>  # optional, if it exists
//
// A short-form file has:
//
//   // ## Tokenomics
//   //
//   //   canonical:    no   # this file is the compressed form
//
// If `canonical: yes`, this file is the long-form. If `canonical: no`, this file is itself the compressed form and points to its canonical sibling.

// ## 3. When to use canonical
//
// Use canonical when:
//   - A human reviews the doc.
//   - Writing documentation or a tutorial.
//   - First-pass review of new content.
//   - Full fidelity is required (comments, asserts, naming).

// ## 4. When to use short-form
//
// Use short-form when:
//   - LLM context and token savings matter.
//   - Bulk migration across many files.
//   - One-shot read for a single specific answer.

// ## 5. Compression rules
//
// Short-form is produced by:
//   - 1-2 char identifiers for locals (e.g. `tc`, `uv`).
//   - Shorten comments to a single line per block.
//   - Drop inline asserts.
//   - Combine related lines.
//
// Preserved: public API, type signatures, `.oe` files.
// Stripped: private helper bodies in full, inline asserts, multi-line comments.

// ## 6. Example
//
// Canonical:
//   pub fn typecheck_undefined_vars(toks: List[String]) -> List[String]
//
// Short-form:
//   pub fn tc_uv(toks: List) -> List

// ## 7. Trade-offs
//
// Short-form is brittle:
//   - Harder to read — names are opaque.
//   - Harder to maintain — a rename in canonical may not propagate.
//   - Lower fidelity — dropped asserts hide invariants.
//
// Default to canonical. Reach for short-form only when tokens are the bottleneck.
